{"timeLimit":7200,"questions":[{"question":"Simplify: \\(\\dfrac{(0.4)^{2} + (0.45) + (0.6)^{2}}{(0.68)^{2} - (0.29)^{2}}\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{24}{60}\\)","\\(\\frac{68}{28}\\)","\\(\\frac{72}{74}\\)","\\(\\frac{120}{25}\\)","\\(\\frac{100}{39}\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Top: \\(0.16 + 0.45 + 0.36 = 0.97\\).","Bottom, a difference of two squares: \\((0.68 + 0.29)(0.68 - 0.29) = 0.97 \\times 0.39\\).","\\(\\frac{0.97}{0.97 \\times 0.39} = \\frac{1}{0.39} = \\frac{100}{39}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Approximation","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(b > 0\\), \\(\\frac{a}{b} = -\\frac{4}{3}\\) and \\(a + b = c\\), which of the following is correct?","options":["\\(a < b < c\\)","\\(a < c < b\\)","\\(b < c < a\\)","\\(b < a < c\\)","\\(c < a < b\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(a = -\\frac{4}{3}b\\) is negative, and \\(c = a + b = -\\frac{1}{3}b\\) is negative but closer to \\(0\\).","So \\(a < c < b\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Inequalities","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify: \\(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left[2 - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right] + 1\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)","\\(-\\frac{4}{3}\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{6}\\)","\\(\\frac{2}{3}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{6}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Inside first: \\(1 + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\\), half of it is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\), and \\(2 - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\\).","\\(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} + 1 = \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} + 1 = \\frac{2}{3}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Fractions","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify: \\(\\left[2 - \\cfrac{1}{1 - \\cfrac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}}}\\right] \\cdot \\left[1 + \\cfrac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{3}}\\right]\\)","options":["\\(-3\\)","\\(7\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)","\\(9\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["First bracket: \\(1 - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{2/3} = \\frac{3}{2}\\), \\(1 - \\frac{3}{2} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{-1/2} = -2\\), so \\(2 - (-2) = 4\\).","Second: \\(1 + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}\\).","\\(4 \\times \\frac{7}{4} = 7\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Fractions","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If the sum of two numbers is \\(55\\) and the HCF and LCM of these two numbers are \\(5\\) and \\(120\\) respectively, what is the sum of the reciprocals of the two numbers?","options":["\\(\\frac{55}{601}\\)","\\(\\frac{601}{55}\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{120}\\)","\\(\\frac{120}{11}\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{600}\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The product of two numbers is their HCF times their LCM: \\(5 \\times 120 = 600\\).","\\(\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{a + b}{ab} = \\frac{55}{600} = \\frac{11}{120}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"LCM and HCF","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Find the greatest common factor of \\(20d^{2}(d - 2)^{4}\\), \\(10(d - 2)^{5}\\) and \\(10d(d - 2)^{7}\\).","options":["\\(20(d - 2)^{5}\\)","\\(10(d - 2)^{5}\\)","\\(20(d - 2)^{4}\\)","\\(10(d - 2)^{4}\\)","\\(10(d - 2)^{3}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The numbers share \\(10\\); \\(d\\) is not in the second term; the lowest power of \\((d - 2)\\) is \\(4\\).","GCF \\(= 10(d - 2)^{4}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"LCM and HCF","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify \\(\\left(\\dfrac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{-2} - 5(-2)^{-2} + 6^{-1}}{1 + 2^{-2}} - \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2}\\right)^{-1}\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{11}{5}\\)","\\(-\\frac{7}{3}\\)","\\(-\\frac{3}{20}\\)","\\(\\frac{3}{20}\\)","\\(-\\frac{20}{3}\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Top: \\(4 - \\frac{5}{4} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{48 - 15 + 2}{12} = \\frac{35}{12}\\); bottom: \\(\\frac{5}{4}\\); so the fraction is \\(\\frac{35}{12} \\times \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{7}{3}\\).","\\(\\frac{7}{3} - 9 = -\\frac{20}{3}\\), and its \\(-1\\) power is \\(-\\frac{3}{20}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Indices","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Solve for \\(x\\) in \\(\\sqrt{2 \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2}}} = \\sqrt[4]{2^{x}}\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{7}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{9}{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Inside out: \\(4 \\cdot 2^{1/4} = 2^{9/4}\\), its cube root is \\(2^{3/4}\\), times \\(2\\) is \\(2^{7/4}\\), and the square root is \\(2^{7/8}\\).","\\(\\frac{x}{4} = \\frac{7}{8}\\), so \\(x = \\frac{7}{2}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Indices","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Two equilateral triangles have sides of lengths \\(38\\) and \\(114\\) respectively. The greatest length of tape that can measure both of them exactly is formed. How many such equal parts can be measured?","options":["\\(20\\)","\\(24\\)","\\(27\\)","\\(31\\)","\\(12\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The greatest length that measures both sides exactly is the HCF: \\(38\\).","All six sides together: \\(3 \\times 38 + 3 \\times 114 = 456\\), which is \\(456 \\div 38 = 12\\) lengths of tape.","(None of the printed options is \\(12\\); it replaces option E, \\(35\\).)"],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"LCM and HCF","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The L.C.M. of \\(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{4}{7}\\) and \\(\\frac{9}{13}\\) is equal to","options":["\\(36\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{36}\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{1365}\\)","\\(\\frac{12}{455}\\)","\\(\\frac{36}{455}\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["L.C.M. of fractions \\(= \\frac{\\text{L.C.M. of the tops}}{\\text{H.C.F. of the bottoms}} = \\frac{36}{1} = 36\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"LCM and HCF","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(463 = (abcd)_{5}\\) where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) are digits, what is \\(a + b + c + d\\)?","options":["\\(15\\)","\\(14\\)","\\(13\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(11\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(463 = 3 \\times 125 + 3 \\times 25 + 2 \\times 5 + 3\\), so \\(463 = (3323)_{5}\\).","\\(3 + 3 + 2 + 3 = 11\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Number Bases","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Solve for \\(x\\) in \\((1011)_{2} + (101)_{2} = x_{5}\\)","options":["\\(13\\)","\\(16\\)","\\(31\\)","\\(61\\)","\\(69\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(1011_{2} = 11\\) and \\(101_{2} = 5\\), so the sum is \\(16\\).","\\(16 = 3 \\times 5 + 1 = (31)_{5}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Number Bases","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify: \\(\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\cdots \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\right)\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{3}{n}\\)","\\(\\frac{n - 1}{n}\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{n}\\)","\\(\\frac{2}{n}\\)","\\(\\frac{3}{2n}\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(\\frac{3}{4} \\times \\frac{4}{5} \\times \\frac{5}{6} \\times \\cdots \\times \\frac{n - 1}{n}\\): everything cancels except \\(\\frac{3}{n}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Sequences","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(\\dfrac{32}{11a} = 1.\\overline{45}\\), what is the value of \\(a\\)?","options":["\\(5\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(1\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(1.\\overline{45} = 1 + \\frac{45}{99} = 1 + \\frac{5}{11} = \\frac{16}{11}\\).","\\(\\frac{32}{11a} = \\frac{16}{11}\\), so \\(a = 2\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Recurring Decimals","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Evaluate: \\(1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + 5 - 6 + \\cdots + 71 - 72 + 73\\)","options":["\\(36\\)","\\(37\\)","\\(73\\)","\\(145\\)","\\(189\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Each pair \\((1 - 2), (3 - 4), \\ldots, (71 - 72)\\) is \\(-1\\): \\(36\\) pairs give \\(-36\\).","\\(-36 + 73 = 37\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Series","type":"objective","section":null,"film":"seq-alt"},{"question":"What is the solution set of the quadratic equation \\(2x^{2} - 9x + 7 = 0\\)?","options":["\\(\\{-\\frac{7}{2}, 1\\}\\)","\\(\\{\\frac{7}{2}, -1\\}\\)","\\(\\{7, -\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\)","\\(\\{\\frac{7}{2}, 1\\}\\)","\\(\\{-\\frac{7}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(2x^{2} - 9x + 7 = (2x - 7)(x - 1) = 0\\).","\\(x = \\frac{7}{2}\\) or \\(x = 1\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Quadratics","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Given that \\(x^{2} - 5x + 4 = 0\\) and \\(x_{1} > x_{2}\\), which one of the following is equal to \\(x_{1}\\sqrt{x_{2}} + x_{2}\\sqrt{x_{1}}\\)?","options":["\\(5\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(4\\sqrt{7}\\)","\\(1\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\((x - 4)(x - 1) = 0\\), so \\(x_{1} = 4\\), \\(x_{2} = 1\\).","\\(4\\sqrt{1} + 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{4} = 4 + 2 = 6\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Quadratics","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(x - z = z - y = 3\\), evaluate \\(x^{2} + y^{2} - 2z^{2}\\).","options":["\\(6\\)","\\(9\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(15\\)","\\(18\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(x = z + 3\\) and \\(y = z - 3\\).","\\((z + 3)^{2} + (z - 3)^{2} - 2z^{2} = 18\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Algebraic Identities","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify: \\(\\left(\\dfrac{x^{2} + xy}{xy + y^{2}} - \\dfrac{xy - y^{2}}{x^{2} - xy}\\right) : \\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} - \\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\\)","options":["\\(x\\)","\\(x - y\\)","\\(y\\)","\\(x + y\\)","\\(\\frac{x - y}{x + y}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(\\frac{x(x + y)}{y(x + y)} = \\frac{x}{y}\\) and \\(\\frac{y(x - y)}{x(x - y)} = \\frac{y}{x}\\); their difference is \\(\\frac{x^{2} - y^{2}}{xy}\\).","Divide by \\(\\frac{x - y}{xy}\\): \\(\\frac{(x - y)(x + y)}{xy} \\times \\frac{xy}{x - y} = x + y\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Algebraic Fractions","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(\\sqrt{a} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}} = 2\\), evaluate \\(a^{2} + \\frac{1}{a^{2}}\\).","options":["\\(4\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(18\\)","\\(34\\)","\\(36\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Square: \\(a - 2 + \\frac{1}{a} = 4\\), so \\(a + \\frac{1}{a} = 6\\).","Square again: \\(a^{2} + 2 + \\frac{1}{a^{2}} = 36\\), so \\(a^{2} + \\frac{1}{a^{2}} = 34\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Algebraic Identities","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(2m789n\\) is a six-digit number divisible by \\(3\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are digits, which of the following can be \\(m + n\\)?","options":["\\(2, 4, 6\\)","\\(3, 6, 9\\)","\\(1, 4, 7\\)","\\(5, 8, 2\\)","\\(1, 5, 7\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The digit sum \\(2 + 7 + 8 + 9 + m + n = 26 + m + n\\) must be a multiple of \\(3\\).","So \\(m + n\\) is \\(1, 4, 7, \\ldots\\) (one more than a multiple of \\(3\\))."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Divisibility","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(a : b = 3 : 7\\) and \\(b : c = 9 : 5\\). What is \\(a : b : c\\)?","options":["\\(3 : 15 : 5\\)","\\(21 : 16 : 45\\)","\\(3 : 7 : 5\\)","\\(27 : 63 : 35\\)","\\(27 : 62 : 38\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Make \\(b\\) the same: \\(a : b = 27 : 63\\) and \\(b : c = 63 : 35\\).","\\(a : b : c = 27 : 63 : 35\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Ratio","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(\\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{z}{t} = 5\\), evaluate \\(\\left(\\frac{x + y}{y}\\right)\\left(\\frac{z + t}{z}\\right)\\)","options":["\\(3\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(10\\)","\\(\\frac{36}{5}\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(\\frac{x + y}{y} = \\frac{x}{y} + 1 = 6\\) and \\(\\frac{z + t}{z} = 1 + \\frac{t}{z} = \\frac{6}{5}\\).","\\(6 \\times \\frac{6}{5} = \\frac{36}{5}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Ratio","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(w\\) varies inversely as \\(v\\) and \\(u\\) varies directly as \\(w^{3}\\), find the relationship between \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) given that \\(u = 1\\) when \\(v = 2\\).","options":["\\(u = 8v^{3}\\)","\\(u = 2^{3}\\sqrt{v}\\)","\\(v = \\frac{8}{u^{3}}\\)","\\(u = \\frac{8}{v^{3}}\\)","\\(u = \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{v^{3}}}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(w = \\frac{k}{v}\\) and \\(u = cw^{3}\\), so \\(u = \\frac{K}{v^{3}}\\).","\\(1 = \\frac{K}{8}\\), so \\(K = 8\\): \\(u = \\frac{8}{v^{3}}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Variation","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(2x + y = z\\), \\(x + y + z = 12\\) and \\(x + z = 3y\\), solve for \\(z\\).","options":["\\(3\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(7\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Put \\(x + z = 3y\\) into the second: \\(4y = 12\\), so \\(y = 3\\) and \\(x + z = 9\\).","\\(z = 2x + 3\\): \\(x + 2x + 3 = 9\\), \\(x = 2\\), \\(z = 7\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Simultaneous Equations","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Given that \\(p^{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{q}}{r}\\), make \\(q\\) the subject of the equation.","options":["\\(q = p\\sqrt{r}\\)","\\(q = p^{3}r\\)","\\(q = pr^{3}\\)","\\(q = pr^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\)","\\(q = r\\sqrt{p}\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Cube both sides: \\(p = \\frac{q}{r^{3}}\\).","\\(q = pr^{3}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Change of Subject","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are two positive numbers such that \\(p > 2q\\). Which one of the following statements is not true?","options":["\\(-p < -2q\\)","\\(-p > -2q\\)","\\(-q < 2p\\)","\\(q < \\frac{1}{2}p\\)","\\(p^{2} > 2q^{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Multiplying \\(p > 2q\\) by \\(-1\\) turns it round: \\(-p < -2q\\). So B is false.","The others all follow from \\(p > 2q > 0\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Inequalities","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(x\\) varies over the set of real numbers. Which of the following is illustrated in the diagram?","options":["\\(-2 \\le x < 3\\)","\\(-2 < x \\le 3\\)","\\(-2 < x < 3\\)","\\(-2 \\le x \\le 3\\)","\\(x \\ge 3\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Both circles are filled in, so both ends are included: \\(-2 \\le x \\le 3\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q28.svg","topic":"Inequalities","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Find the probability that a number selected at random from \\(40\\) to \\(50\\) is a prime.","options":["\\(\\frac{3}{11}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{11}\\)","\\(\\frac{3}{10}\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{11}\\)","\\(\\frac{6}{11}\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["From \\(40\\) to \\(50\\) there are \\(11\\) numbers; the primes are \\(41, 43, 47\\).","\\(\\frac{3}{11}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Probability","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(x * y = x + y^{2}\\), find the value of \\((2 * 3) * 5\\).","options":["\\(55\\)","\\(36\\)","\\(25\\)","\\(11\\)","\\(16\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(2 * 3 = 2 + 9 = 11\\).","\\(11 * 5 = 11 + 25 = 36\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Operations","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In a family of \\(21\\) people, the average age is \\(14\\) years. If the age of the grandfather is not counted, the average age drops to \\(12\\) years. What is the age of the grandfather?","options":["\\(35\\)","\\(40\\)","\\(42\\)","\\(52\\)","\\(54\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Total: \\(21 \\times 14 = 294\\). Without him: \\(20 \\times 12 = 240\\).","Grandfather: \\(294 - 240 = 54\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Averages","type":"objective","section":null,"film":"avg-grandfather"},{"question":"Find the sum of the range and the mode of the set of numbers \\(10, 5, 10, 9, 8, 7, 7, 10, 8, 4, 6, 9, 10, 9, 10, 9, 7, 10, 6, 5\\).","options":["\\(16\\)","\\(14\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(10\\)","\\(8\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The mode is \\(10\\) (six times). The range is \\(10 - 4 = 6\\).","\\(10 + 6 = 16\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Statistics","type":"objective","section":null,"film":"avg-range-mode"},{"question":"Evaluate: \\(\\dfrac{15! + 14!}{15! - 14!}\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{14}{13}\\)","\\(\\frac{8}{7}\\)","\\(\\frac{15}{14}\\)","\\(\\frac{15}{4}\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{3}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(15! = 15 \\times 14!\\), so this is \\(\\frac{14!(15 + 1)}{14!(15 - 1)} = \\frac{16}{14} = \\frac{8}{7}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Factorials","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In how many distinct ways can the letters of the word BALLOON be arranged?","options":["\\(840\\)","\\(1260\\)","\\(2520\\)","\\(5040\\)","\\(5890\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(7\\) letters, with L twice and O twice: \\(\\frac{7!}{2!\\,2!} = \\frac{5040}{4} = 1260\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Permutations","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In a class of \\(120\\) students, \\(18\\) of them scored an A grade in Mathematics. If the section representing the A grade students on a pie chart has angle \\(Z^{\\circ}\\) at the centre of the circle, what is \\(Z\\)?","options":["\\(15\\)","\\(27\\)","\\(50\\)","\\(54\\)","\\(60\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(\\frac{18}{120} \\times 360^{\\circ} = 54^{\\circ}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Statistics","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Evaluate: \\(\\sin 45^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 60^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cot 60^{\\circ} \\cdot \\tan 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\tan 60^{\\circ}\\)","options":["\\(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\)","\\(\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}\\)","\\(16\\)","\\(-16\\)","\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(\\cot 60^{\\circ} \\cdot \\tan 60^{\\circ} = 1\\), leaving \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\).","\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{8} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Trigonometry","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"From the figure, calculate \\(TH\\) in centimetres.","options":["\\(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)","\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\)","\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Let \\(TH = h\\). The \\(45^{\\circ}\\) angle gives \\(QH = h\\); the \\(30^{\\circ}\\) angle gives \\(PH = h\\sqrt{3}\\).","\\(PQ = h\\sqrt{3} - h = 5\\), so \\(h = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q37.svg","topic":"Trigonometry","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{5}\\) and \\(1 + \\tan^{2} x = a\\), find \\(a\\).","options":["\\(25\\)","\\(30\\)","\\(35\\)","\\(40\\)","\\(45\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(1 + \\tan^{2} x = \\sec^{2} x = \\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x} = 25\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Trigonometry","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(\\frac{|AB|}{|BC|} = \\frac{3}{4}\\) as in the line below, what is the value of \\(y - x\\)?","options":["\\(\\frac{13}{4}\\)","\\(\\frac{16}{3}\\)","\\(16\\)","\\(17\\)","\\(\\frac{39}{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["From \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) is a step of \\((-1, 5)\\). \\(BC\\) is \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\) as long, in the same direction: \\(\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}, \\frac{20}{3}\\right)\\).","\\(C = \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{47}{3}\\right)\\), so \\(y - x = \\frac{47}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} = 16\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q39.svg","topic":"Coordinate Geometry","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Two \\(4 \\times 4\\) squares intersect at right angles, bisecting their intersecting sides, as shown. The circle's diameter is the segment between the two points of intersection. What is the area of the shaded region created by removing the circle from the squares?","options":["\\(16 - 4\\pi\\)","\\(16 - 2\\pi\\)","\\(28 - 4\\pi\\)","\\(28 - 2\\pi\\)","\\(32 - 2\\pi\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The squares overlap in a \\(2 \\times 2\\) square, so together they cover \\(16 + 16 - 4 = 28\\).","The two crossing points are opposite corners of that small square: the diameter is \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\), the radius \\(\\sqrt{2}\\), and the circle's area \\(2\\pi\\).","\\(28 - 2\\pi\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q40.svg","topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Three angles of a nonagon are equal and the sum of the six other angles is \\(1110^{\\circ}\\). Calculate the size of one of the equal angles.","options":["\\(210^{\\circ}\\)","\\(150^{\\circ}\\)","\\(105^{\\circ}\\)","\\(50^{\\circ}\\)","\\(25^{\\circ}\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["A nonagon's angles add up to \\((9 - 2) \\times 180^{\\circ} = 1260^{\\circ}\\).","\\(1260^{\\circ} - 1110^{\\circ} = 150^{\\circ}\\) for the three, so each is \\(50^{\\circ}\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Polygons","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Find the perimeter of a square if the length of its diagonal is \\(12\\) cm.","options":["\\(12\\sqrt{2}\\)","\\(24\\sqrt{2}\\)","\\(36\\sqrt{2}\\)","\\(48\\sqrt{2}\\)","\\(54\\sqrt{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Side \\(= \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{2}} = 6\\sqrt{2}\\); perimeter \\(= 24\\sqrt{2}\\) cm."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In the given figure \\(AK \\parallel CL\\), \\(m(\\angle A) = 8x\\), \\(m(\\angle B) = 132^{\\circ}\\), \\(m(\\angle C) = 4x\\). Find \\(m(\\angle BCL)\\).","options":["\\(44\\)","\\(88\\)","\\(126\\)","\\(136\\)","\\(144\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(CL\\) leans at \\(4x\\) to the line through \\(B\\) and \\(C\\); \\(AK\\) is parallel, and \\(BA\\) is \\(132^{\\circ}\\) round from \\(BC\\).","The angle at \\(A\\) is the difference: \\(132^{\\circ} - 4x = 8x\\), so \\(x = 11^{\\circ}\\).","\\(\\angle BCL = 180^{\\circ} - 4x = 136^{\\circ}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q43.svg","topic":"Parallel Lines","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In the given figure \\(d_{1} \\parallel d_{2}\\), find \\(m + n\\).","options":["\\(4(a + b)\\)","\\(\\frac{a + b}{4}\\)","\\(a + b\\)","\\(\\frac{a - b}{4}\\)","\\(\\frac{2a + b}{4}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Where a zigzag between parallel lines bends, the bend angle equals the sum of the two angles it makes with the lines: \\(a = n + 2m\\) and \\(b = 3n + 2m\\).","\\(a + b = 4n + 4m\\), so \\(m + n = \\frac{a + b}{4}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q44.svg","topic":"Parallel Lines","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In the given triangle \\(ABC\\), \\(DC \\perp AB\\) and \\(AC \\perp BE\\). If \\(m(\\angle BAC) = 75^{\\circ}\\) and \\(m(\\angle CBE) = 45^{\\circ}\\), find \\(m(\\angle BCD) = x\\).","options":["\\(20\\)","\\(25\\)","\\(30\\)","\\(45\\)","\\(60\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["In right-angled triangle \\(ABE\\): \\(\\angle ABE = 90^{\\circ} - 75^{\\circ} = 15^{\\circ}\\), so \\(\\angle ABC = 15^{\\circ} + 45^{\\circ} = 60^{\\circ}\\).","In right-angled triangle \\(BCD\\): \\(x = 90^{\\circ} - 60^{\\circ} = 30^{\\circ}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q45.svg","topic":"Triangles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(ABCD\\) is a trapezium, \\(m(\\angle ABC) = ax\\), \\(m(\\angle DAB) = x\\), \\(m(\\angle BCD) = 3x\\), \\(m(\\angle CDA) = bx\\). Find the value of \\(b - a\\).","options":["\\(2\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(6\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(AB \\parallel DC\\), so the angles along each slanted side add up to \\(180^{\\circ}\\): \\(x + bx = 180^{\\circ}\\) and \\(ax + 3x = 180^{\\circ}\\).","So \\(1 + b = a + 3\\), and \\(b - a = 2\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q46.svg","topic":"Polygons","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(ABCD\\) is a rectangle. If \\((a - b) = 6\\) cm and \\(|AC| = 16\\) cm, find Area\\((ABCD)\\).","options":["\\(75\\)","\\(110\\)","\\(128\\)","\\(146\\)","\\(160\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(a^{2} + b^{2} = 16^{2} = 256\\) and \\((a - b)^{2} = 36\\).","\\(2ab = 256 - 36 = 220\\), so the area \\(ab = 110\\ \\text{cm}^{2}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q47.svg","topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The volume of a cylinder is \\(1200\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\) and the area of its base is \\(150\\ \\text{cm}^{2}\\). Find the height of the cylinder.","options":["\\(80.0\\) cm","\\(8.0\\) cm","\\(0.80\\) cm","\\(0.08\\) cm","\\(0.008\\) cm"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Volume \\(=\\) base area \\(\\times\\) height: \\(h = \\frac{1200}{150} = 8.0\\) cm."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What is the volume of the regular three-dimensional figure drawn below?","options":["\\(160\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)","\\(48\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)","\\(96\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)","\\(120\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)","\\(140\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The end is a right-angled triangle with \\(PQ = 5\\) and \\(QV = RT = 4\\), so \\(PV = 3\\) and its area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times 3 = 6\\).","Volume \\(= 6 \\times 8 = 48\\ \\text{cm}^{3}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q49.svg","topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In the figure, the line segment \\(ST\\) is tangent to the two circles at \\(S\\) and \\(T\\). \\(O\\) and \\(Q\\) are the centres of the circles, with \\(OS = 5\\) cm, \\(QT = 2\\) cm and \\(OQ = 14\\) cm. Find \\(ST\\).","options":["\\(7\\sqrt{3}\\) cm","\\(12\\) cm","\\(\\sqrt{87}\\) cm","\\(7\\) cm","\\(\\sqrt{3}\\) cm"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The tangent crosses between the circles, so slide \\(QT\\) across to the far side of \\(OS\\): a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse \\(OQ = 14\\) and one side \\(5 + 2 = 7\\).","\\(ST = \\sqrt{14^{2} - 7^{2}} = \\sqrt{147} = 7\\sqrt{3}\\) cm."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q50.svg","topic":"Circle Geometry","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(x + y + z + t + v + q + m = 245\\) from the graph below, what is \\(m - x + y + z\\)?","options":["\\(50\\)","\\(60\\)","\\(70\\)","\\(80\\)","\\(90\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The graph reads \\(9, 3, 5, 13, 7, 1, 11\\) in some unit, \\(49\\) units in all. \\(49\\) units \\(= 245\\), so one unit is \\(5\\).","\\(m - x + y + z = (11 - 9 + 3 + 5) \\times 5 = 50\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q51.svg","topic":"Graphs","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"John is the brother of Peter. Mary is the mother of John. David is the father of Mary. What is the relationship between David and Peter?","options":["Grandfather","Father","Uncle","Son","Brother"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Mary is the mother of both brothers, and David is Mary's father: David is Peter's grandfather."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Reasoning","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Given the figure below, what is the value of \\(\\sqrt{x}\\)?","options":["\\(0\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(4\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(6\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Second table: \\(a + b = 14\\), \\(b + c = 10\\), \\(c + a = 12\\), so \\(a + b + c = 18\\): \\(a = 8\\), \\(b = 6\\), \\(c = 4\\).","First table: \\(\\sqrt{x} + \\sqrt{y} = a - b = 2\\), \\(\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{z} = a + c = 12\\), \\(\\sqrt{z} + \\sqrt{x} = b + c = 10\\).","Half of their sum is \\(12\\), so \\(\\sqrt{x} = 12 - 12 = 0\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q53.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"A big cube is made up of \\(125\\) small cubes. All the faces of the big cube are then painted. How many of the small cubes have exactly one painted face?","options":["\\(24\\)","\\(36\\)","\\(54\\)","\\(150\\)","None"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The big cube is \\(5 \\times 5 \\times 5\\). On each face, the small cubes not on an edge form a \\(3 \\times 3\\) square: \\(9\\).","\\(6 \\times 9 = 54\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Counting","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(a \\circledast b = 2a + b^{2}\\), \\(a * b = a^{2} - b^{2}\\) and \\(a \\,\\triangle\\, b = \\frac{a \\cdot b}{3}\\), what is \\((3 \\circledast 4) \\,\\triangle\\, (4 * 1)\\)?","options":["\\(110\\)","\\(66\\)","\\(33\\)","\\(77\\)","\\(88\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(3 \\circledast 4 = 6 + 16 = 22\\) and \\(4 * 1 = 16 - 1 = 15\\).","\\(22 \\,\\triangle\\, 15 = \\frac{22 \\times 15}{3} = 110\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Operations","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What number should replace the question mark in the figure below?","options":["\\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{5}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{7}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{9}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{2}\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Each shape gives (number of parts) \\(\\div\\) (shaded parts) \\(+\\) (white parts): I is \\(\\frac{8}{3} + 5\\); II is \\(\\frac{8}{4} + 4 = 6\\).","III: \\(5\\) parts, \\(2\\) shaded, \\(3\\) white: \\(\\frac{5}{2} + 3 = \\frac{11}{2}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q56.svg","topic":"Visual Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In a row of trees, a tree is 7th from the left and 14th from the right end. How many trees are there in the row?","options":["\\(18\\)","\\(19\\)","\\(20\\)","\\(21\\)","\\(22\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(6\\) trees to its left, \\(13\\) to its right, and the tree itself: \\(6 + 13 + 1 = 20\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Reasoning","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What number should replace the question mark in the figure below?","options":["\\(5\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(7\\)","\\(8\\)","\\(9\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Add the four corner numbers and write the total in the two middle triangles: \\(9 + 7 + 12 + 15 = 43\\) gives \\(4\\) and \\(3\\); \\(8 + 4 + 18 + 21 = 51\\) gives \\(5\\) and \\(1\\).","\\(30 + 47 + 4 + 7 = 88\\): the missing number is \\(8\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q58.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Three jugs, X, Y and Z, contain some liquid. Jug X has \\(400\\) mL liquid, Jug Y has \\(500\\) mL liquid and Jug Z has a total capacity of \\(1\\) litre. Kim pours liquid from jugs X and Y into jug Z, until jug Z is full. Afterwards, how much liquid is left in jugs X and Y altogether?","options":["\\(100\\) mL","\\(150\\) mL","\\(200\\) mL","\\(300\\) mL","\\(650\\) mL"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Jug Z's four marks split its \\(1000\\) mL into quarters, and it already holds one quarter: \\(250\\) mL. It needs \\(750\\) mL more.","X and Y hold \\(900\\) mL together: \\(900 - 750 = 150\\) mL is left."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q59.svg","topic":"Measurement","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Given the figure below, what is the value of \\(x + y\\)?","options":["\\(77\\)","\\(76\\)","\\(75\\)","\\(74\\)","\\(73\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Right side: the circle is \\(30 + 5 = 35\\); \\(35 \\div 5 = 7\\); \\(7 + 9 = 16\\); \\(y = 16 \\times 2 = 32\\).","Bottom: \\(30 \\times 2 = 60\\); \\(x = 60 - 15 = 45\\) (check: \\(45 \\div 9 = 5\\), \\(5 + 11 = 16\\)).","\\(x + y = 77\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q60.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"A man has \\(12\\) sons and each of his sons has \\(1\\) sister. How many children has the man?","options":["\\(24\\)","\\(25\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(13\\)","\\(23\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["All the sons share the same one sister: \\(12 + 1 = 13\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Reasoning","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Which letter comes next in the sequence: B, D, G, K, P, ___?","options":["T","U","V","W","X"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The steps grow by one: \\(+2, +3, +4, +5\\), so next is \\(+6\\).","P \\(+ 6\\) is V."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Sequences","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In a 'magic square', each row, each column and each diagonal add up to the same total. In the magic square below, some of the numbers are missing. What is the missing number at \\(\\blacklozenge\\)?","options":["\\(4\\)","\\(14\\)","\\(17\\)","\\(18\\)","\\(21\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Top row: \\(20 + 15 + 16 = 51\\).","Left column: the middle-left is \\(51 - 38 = 13\\). Diagonal \\(18, ?, 16\\): the centre is \\(17\\). Middle row: \\(51 - 13 - 17 = 21\\).","Right column: \\(51 - 16 - 21 = 14\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q63.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What number should replace the question mark in the figure below?","options":["\\(21\\)","\\(23\\)","\\(24\\)","\\(26\\)","\\(28\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The middle number is the sum of the digits of the other three: \\(7 + 2 + 5 + 7 + 4 + 1 = 26\\), \\(1 + 9 + 2 + 3 + 7 + 1 = 23\\).","\\(4 + 8 + 1 + 1 + 5 + 2 = 21\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q64.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Look at this series: U32, V29, ___, X23, Y20, ... What should fill the blank?","options":["W26","W17","Z17","Z26","None"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The letters run U, V, W, X, Y and the numbers go down by \\(3\\): \\(32, 29, 26, 23, 20\\).","W26."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Sequences","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What number should replace the question mark in the figure below?","options":["\\(10\\)","\\(11\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(13\\)","\\(14\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Each number is the digit sum of the number opposite it: \\(83 \\to 8 + 3 = 11\\), \\(25 \\to 7\\), \\(92 \\to 11\\), \\(98 \\to 17\\), \\(85 \\to 13\\).","Opposite the question mark is \\(77\\): \\(7 + 7 = 14\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q66.svg","topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Given \\(AB \\times BA\\), with partial products \\(D0B\\) and \\(5DD\\) adding to \\(58D8\\), what is the value of \\(A + B + D\\)?","options":["\\(18\\)","\\(20\\)","\\(22\\)","\\(24\\)","\\(26\\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\(AB \\times A = D0B\\) and \\(AB \\times B = 5DD\\). Trying digits, only \\(68\\) works: \\(68 \\times 6 = 408\\) and \\(68 \\times 8 = 544\\).","\\(68 \\times 86 = 5848\\). So \\(A = 6\\), \\(B = 8\\), \\(D = 4\\): \\(18\\)."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Number Puzzles","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What five-letter word becomes shorter when you add two letters to it?","options":["Small","Short","Tiny","Large","There is no such word"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Short \\(+\\) er \\(=\\) shorter."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Reasoning","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What is the value of the perimeter of the figure below?","options":["\\(112y\\)","\\(114y\\)","\\(116y\\)","\\(118y\\)","\\(120y\\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["Going across, every upright line meets the outline twice: the flat sides add up to \\(2 \\times 32y = 64y\\).","Upright sides: the left block's \\(12y\\), its right side above and below the band \\(4y + 4y\\), the band's end \\(4y\\), and each tall piece, as tall as the block, shows \\(12y - 4y = 8y\\) on each side: \\(4 \\times 8y = 32y\\).","\\(64y + 12y + 8y + 4y + 32y = 120y\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q69.svg","topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\(x\\), \\(y\\) and \\(z\\) are positive integers such that the value of \\(x + y\\) is even and the value of \\((x + y)^{2} + x + z\\) is odd, which of the following must be true?","options":["\\(x\\) is odd.","\\(x\\) is even.","If \\(z\\) is even, then \\(x\\) is odd.","If \\(z\\) is even, then \\(xy\\) is even.","\\(xy\\) is even."],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\((x + y)^{2}\\) is even, so \\(x + z\\) must be odd: \\(x\\) and \\(z\\) have opposite parity.","So if \\(z\\) is even, \\(x\\) is odd."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Number Theory","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"A doctor gives you three pills and tells you to take one every half hour. How long will the pills last?","options":["\\(30\\) minutes","\\(45\\) minutes","\\(1\\) hour","\\(1.5\\) hours","\\(2\\) hours"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["One at once, one after \\(30\\) minutes, one after \\(60\\) minutes: \\(1\\) hour."],"hint":"","image":null,"topic":"Reasoning","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"On the number line below, there are \\(9\\) equal intervals between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). What is the value of \\(x\\)?","options":["\\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{2}{3}\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{9}\\)","\\(\\frac{9}{2}\\)","\\(\\frac{9}{4}\\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The arrow is at the 6th of \\(9\\) marks: \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{6}{9} = \\frac{2}{3}\\).","\\(x = \\frac{4}{9}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q72.svg","topic":"Number Line","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The shaded area in the figure below is ___ \\(x^{2}\\).","options":["\\(56\\)","\\(58\\)","\\(60\\)","\\(62\\)","\\(64\\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The right-hand part is whole squares: \\(4 + 18 + 4 + 4 = 30\\).","The left-hand part has slanted edges; pairing the half squares (or using the corners' coordinates) gives \\(32\\).","\\(30 + 32 = 62\\ x^{2}\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q73.svg","topic":"Mensuration","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What graph should replace the question mark in the figure below?","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The table puts M with the square, N with the triangle, S with the crossed circle, R with the dot and Q with the ring.","On the graph's scale that is low, high, middle, lower, top: graph D."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q74.svg","topic":"Graphs","type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\(X + Y + Z + T = 2300\\). What is \\(Z - T\\)?","options":["\\(220\\)","\\(230\\)","\\(240\\)","\\(250\\)","\\(260\\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["The bars read \\(20, 10, 40, 30\\): \\(100\\) units for \\(2300\\), so one unit is \\(23\\).","\\(Z - T = (40 - 30) \\times 23 = 230\\)."],"hint":"","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/anmc/junior-secondary/22nd/first-round/q75.svg","topic":"Graphs","type":"objective","section":null}]}